{#
  Container-level `securityContext` for a hardened k8s container — the
  `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false` + `capabilities.drop: [ALL]` body that ~100 container specs
  across the fleet share, varying only in which of four optional keys they set and which
  capabilities they add back.

  A container-level securityContext is a DIFFERENT object from a pod-level one, and only the
  container-level one governs capabilities — see ansible/tests/k8s/test_container_security_context.py
  for why that distinction matters. This macro emits the
  container-level block only; a pod-level `securityContext` (fsGroup, supplementalGroups,
  sysctls) stays written out at its call site.

  Indentation is fixed at 10 spaces, which is the container-list depth in a Deployment,
  DaemonSet, StatefulSet or CronJob pod template here (100 of the fleet's 102 blocks). The two
  that sit deeper — a CronJob's `jobTemplate` nests one level further — are deliberately NOT
  converted rather than given an indent argument, because a macro that can emit at two depths
  is one that can emit at the wrong one.

  NOT FOR `runAsUser: 0`. A root container that drops ALL and adds no DAC capability is weaker
  than the pod's own uid, because it can no longer read another uid's files — so every root site
  here carries a comment naming which uid owns the files, at what mode, and why root is reached
  for at all. A one-line macro call has nowhere to put that, and the capabilities stay written
  literally so a grep for `DAC_OVERRIDE` finds every container granted it. code-server's
  deployment.yaml.j2, loki-homelab's alloy-daemonset.yaml.j2 and the CrowdSec seeding init
  containers in crowdsec-agent.yml.j2 (rendered into authelia's and traefik's pods) are the
  `runAsUser: 0` sites in this family and stay written out in full.
  ansible/tests/k8s/test_root_security_context_is_written_out_in_full.py refuses a macro call
  that passes `run_as_user=0`, and
  ansible/tests/k8s/test_root_needs_dac_capability.py's
  test_every_documented_root_site_still_renders_a_clean_root_block holds that all six root
  blocks still render. That census reads the RENDER of every role
  template (#3223), so a root block built through this macro is scanned like any other and the
  ban above is a readability convention rather than a way around a blind spot (#3228).

  Arguments:
    read_only      emit `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true`.
    non_root       emit `runAsNonRoot: true`.
    run_as_user    emit `runAsUser: <value>`; omitted when none. Never pass 0 — see above.
    run_as_group   emit `runAsGroup: <value>`; omitted when none.
    add            capabilities to add back, as a list. Written literally at the call site so a
                   grep for a capability name still finds every container that is granted it.

  A key this macro is not asked for is OMITTED, never defaulted — an absent `runAsNonRoot` and
  an explicit `runAsNonRoot: false` are different admission outcomes, and roughly half the fleet
  legitimately leaves it absent.
#}
{% macro hardened_security_context(read_only=false, non_root=false, run_as_user=none, run_as_group=none, add=[]) %}
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
{% if read_only %}
            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
{% endif %}
{% if non_root %}
            runAsNonRoot: true
{% endif %}
{% if run_as_user is not none %}
            runAsUser: {{ run_as_user }}
{% endif %}
{% if run_as_group is not none %}
            runAsGroup: {{ run_as_group }}
{% endif %}
            capabilities:
{% if add %}
              add:
{% for cap in add %}
                - {{ cap }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
              drop:
                - ALL
{%- endmacro %}
